---
title: Presets & history
description: Start from a preset, take it apart, and walk the session back — every operation Latent applies is listed and reversible.
---

## Presets

The Presets panel is a starting point, not a filter: applying one adds its operations to the edit
stack, where every one of them stays yours to change. The library is grouped by intent —
**Essentials**, **Portrait**, **Landscape**, **Cinematic**, **Film**, **Black & white** and
**Detail** — and each preset shows how many operations it carries, so you know what you are about
to inherit.

The Essentials group is the plain-tool end of it: *Punch*, *Recover*, *Flat (grade base)*,
*Soft matte*, *Warm* and *Cool*. *Flat (grade base)* is the one to reach for when you intend to
grade by hand — it sets a neutral base rather than a look.

### Saving your own

**Save these settings as…** turns the current stack into a named preset. Once saved it behaves
like any built-in one: apply it to the next frame, then adjust from there.

## History

Every operation is recorded, in order, in the History panel — adjustments added, values changed,
masks edited, layers reordered, a layer switched off, a preset applied. Applying a preset records
as a single entry that expands into the individual operations it contributed, so a look stays
legible instead of flooding the list.

Select any entry to return the frame to that point. Nothing is destroyed on the way — history is a
record of the edit stack, not a queue of pending changes, and the file on disk is untouched
throughout. The status bar carries a revision counter and undo/redo for step-by-step work.

## Scripting

For the operations you repeat often, Latent has a Python console, toggled with `` Ctrl+` ``. It
addresses the same edit stack the panels do:

```python
latent.photo.develop.exposure = 1.0
latent.photo.stack.add("clarity", amount=20)
```

Changes made from the console land in the history like any other operation, and the panels update
to match. `Ctrl+Enter` runs the buffer.
